---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('postscript')!;
const b = appBySlug('brevo')!;
const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-brevo';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/postscript-vs-brevo');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/postscript-vs-brevo');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Brevo receive Shopify cart events?",
    "answer": "Brevo lists Shopify support for cart_updated, cart_deleted and order_completed. Verify those events in the installed integration before enabling recovery."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Brevo email and Postscript SMS share a basket?",
    "answer": "Only use a shared recovery design after demonstrating identity mapping, purchase exits and dispatch coordination; a shared order feed alone does not prevent duplicate reminders."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should a payment question trigger a coupon?",
    "answer": "Resolve financing, BNPL or bundle configuration first. An incentive does not repair the checkout issue."
  },
  {
    "question": "What does the $280 incentive cost?",
    "answer": "With $162.40 fixed goods cost, a 13% code removes $36.40 and reduces gross profit from $117.60 to $81.20 before other costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Postscript vs Brevo: prove the event-to-sender contract</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Decide whether Brevo owns recovery or works alongside a dedicated Postscript SMS program.</p>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Choose an event owner and a dispatch owner</h2>
<p>This comparison is for a Shopify team considering Brevo recovery email, either as the sequence owner or alongside Postscript SMS. The decision turns on whether the installed store events produce the right basket and stop every participating queue. Do not infer recovery quality from a generic campaign price or a feature score.</p>
<p><a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360021501460-Which-Brevo-plugins-automatically-create-track-events">Brevo’s plugin event table</a> lists Shopify support for cart_updated, cart_deleted and order_completed. That establishes documented integration capability, not successful event delivery in your store. Record one cart identifier across an item addition, quantity change, deletion and completed order. Inspect the actual contact and basket fields in each event.</p>
<p><a href="https://developers.brevo.com/guides/track-website-activity">Brevo’s tracking guide</a> says that a workflow using order_completed as an exit must receive that purchase event. Demonstrate the installed integration before adding custom tracking, and avoid sending the same action twice through plugin and custom code. A test purchase should exit the waiting workflow before dispatch; preserve logs when it does not.</p>
<p><a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564025-understanding-infinity-testing-in-automation-flows">Postscript’s flow documentation</a> distinguishes cart-added and checkout-started triggers and their destination tags, with order-created cancellation. Align the stage being compared: a Brevo cart update and a Postscript checkout start are not identical audience boundaries. This is evidence for mechanics, not a claim about AI lift. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Rehearse mutation, purchase and sender races</h2>
<p>Start the proposed $280 basket with a bundle price that has not applied. Update the offer, change quantity, then reopen the reminder checkout: the shopper should see the corrected basket and valid offer. Next remove the last item and confirm that deletion exits recovery. Complete a separate checkout while both providers have a pending step and inspect both queues. A purchase exit in Brevo is not proof of cancellation in Postscript.</p>
<p>Use the $164 fixture for a repeat customer with an old saved address and a new shipping destination. Check identity mapping and the current cart URL rather than linking a prior checkout. Add a mobile newcomer whose BNPL selection stalls, and route the payment question to support before offering money off. The proposed design needs a named owner for event mapping, another for dispatch if teams differ, and a recorded decision about which sender can act next.</p>
<h2>Channel permission is not acquisition or managed ownership</h2>
<p>If Brevo owns email and Postscript owns SMS, demonstrate email-only and text-only paths as well as dual permission. For crossborder recovery, verify destination support, sending identity, language, channel consent and quiet hours independently. Do not infer SMS permission from email enrollment or international shipping availability. Signup tools acquire an audience; they do not establish who maintains these checks.</p>
<p><a href="https://postscript.io/sales">Postscript’s SMS Sales offering</a> describes human associates engaging SMS subscribers. If that service is proposed, specify reply coverage and when a conversation pauses automation. It is a separate operational scope from Brevo event ingestion or ordinary SMS setup. No automatic Brevo–Postscript send-history sync or managed service inclusion is assumed here.</p>
<h2>Price the offer before choosing its channel</h2>
<p>At $280 and 42% gross margin, goods cost stays $162.40 and full-price gross profit is $117.60. A 13% code costs $36.40, leaving $81.20. At $164 and 55%, fixed goods cost is $73.80 and full-price gross profit is $90.20; an illustrative 16% offer costs $26.24 and leaves $63.96. These retain the original two basket fixtures with explicit fixed goods cost. They are not measured contribution, recommended discounts or evidence that a cheaper message recovers profitably. Deduct shipping subsidies, fees, taxes, returns, messaging and labor for the operating decision.</p>
<h2>Proposed recovery acceptance checks</h2>
<p>These are rehearsal fixtures, not observed merchant results. Start with email-only, SMS-only, dual-subscribed and opted-out contacts. A phone number or an identified browser is not proof of permission for every channel. Record the actual event, consent scope, locale, destination and cancellation state before dispatch. Set timing from the integration delay and permitted sending window, rather than copying a universal three-touch ladder.</p>
<p>For a subscription toggle left on, verify that the return checkout preserves the intended purchase type and makes the recurring terms clear. For a mobile Instagram visitor, reopen the link on the intended device and inspect variants and quantities. For a repeat buyer with a saved address, check the current basket rather than reusing a prior order. When two sizes are unavailable, suppress unavailable items and send a useful sizing or stock answer; stop ordinary recovery for a fully unavailable basket. Confirm that a gift message survives the return trip.</p>
<p>For financing or BNPL abandonment, offer factual payment help without implying approval. Reconcile a missing bundle offer and prevent stacking a welcome code with a recovery code. A below-MOQ B2B order needs a quantity explanation or a named sales owner. An international shipping objection needs a valid destination quote, duties context and currency check before an incentive. A service or refund issue should pause the relevant promotion until its owner releases it.</p>
<h2>Launch and measure one accountable sequence</h2>
<p>Inventory Shopify and every connected sender before enabling replacement recovery. Rebuild the chosen cart and checkout branches with sending disabled, then test purchase during the wait, unsubscribe before dispatch, cart deletion, a newer checkout and duplicate event delivery. Record which queue exits and when. Pause the replaced automations before enabling the new owner; retain suppression records and a rollback plan. If cross-provider message history cannot support reliable coordination, use disjoint cohorts or one recovery owner.</p>
<p>Choose a randomized eligible holdout sized for the decision rather than treating 5% as automatically sufficient. Reconcile unique orders, returns, discounts, goods cost, shipping subsidy, fees, message charges and operating labor. Platform attribution alone does not establish incremental recovery. Report country, currency and channel eligibility separately; do not interpret a change in eligible audience as a conversion improvement.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
